{{nihongo|'''[[:en:Yasakatome|Yasakatome-no-Kami]]'''|八坂刀売神}} is a [[kami]] in [[Shinto]]. She is worshipped in the [[Suwa-taisha|Suwa Taisha]] network of shrines.<ref name="https-8881a">{{Cite web |date=2023-05-22 |title=Suwa Shinkō {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8881 |access-date=2023-12-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230522035213/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8881 |archive-date=2023-05-22 }}</ref> She is the spouse of [[Takeminakata]] and most often considered to be the deity of the Lower Shrine of Suwa or the ''Shimosha''.<ref>Muraoka (1969). pp. 5–6.</ref> Unlike the relatively well-documented Suwa ''Kamisha'', very little concrete information is available regarding the origins of the ''Shimosha'' and its goddess.<ref>Suwa Shishi Hensan Iinkai, ed. (1995). p. 696.</ref>

At Suwa Taisha Takeminakata is enshrined at Hon Miya and Yasakatome is enshrined in Mae Miya.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Gerbert |first=Elaine |date=1996 |title=The Suwa Pillar Festival Revisited |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2719402 |journal=Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies |volume=56 |issue=2 |pages=325 |doi=10.2307/2719402 |jstor=2719402 |issn=0073-0548}}</ref>

She is considered to be the concubine of [[Takeminakata|Takeminakata no Kami]], the enshrined deity of [[Suwa-taisha|Suwa Taisha]] Shrine, and is enshrined at Suwa Taisha Shitasha and Suwa Shrines in various places.<ref name="https-8881a" /> 

Yasakatome's first historical attestation is in the ''[[Shoku Nihon Kōki]]'', where the goddess is given the Rank of junior fifth, lower grade (従五位下) by the imperial court in the tenth month of [[:en:Jōwa (Heian period)|Jōwa]] 9 (842 CE), five months after the same Rank was conferred on Takeminakata.<ref name="Shoku1">{{cite web |title=續日本後紀 |url=http://www.j-texts.com/chuko/shokukouki.html |website=J－TEXTS 日本文学電子図書館}}</ref><ref name="神社資料テータヘース-shinto-jinja-database-2">{{cite web |title=南方刀美神社二座（八坂刀売命神） |url=http://21coe.kokugakuin.ac.jp/db/jinja/kindex2.php?J_ID=240201 |website=神社資料データベース (Shinto Jinja Database) |publisher=Kokugakuin University}}</ref> She is not found in the [[Kojiki]] or the [[Nihon Shoki|Nihonshoki]].<ref name="Shoku1" /><ref name="神社資料テータヘース-shinto-jinja-database-2" /> As Takeminakata rose up in Rank, so did Yasakatome,<ref>Miyasaka (1987). pp. 37-38.</ref><ref name="Montoku">{{cite wikisource|plaintitle=''[[Nihon Montoku Tennō Jitsuroku]]'' (日本文德天皇實錄)|wslink=zh:日本文德天皇實錄|chapter=卷第二}}</ref><ref name="Montoku2">{{cite wikisource|plaintitle=''[[Nihon Montoku Tennō Jitsuroku]]'' (日本文德天皇實錄)|wslink=zh:日本文德天皇實錄|chapter=卷第三}}</ref><ref name="NihonSandai">{{cite wikisource|plaintitle=''[[Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]]'' (日本三代實錄)|wslink=zh:日本三代實錄|chapter=卷第十}}</ref> so that by 867 CE, Yasakatome had been promoted to senior second (正二位).<ref name="NihonSandai2">{{cite wikisource|plaintitle=''[[Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]]'' (日本三代實錄)|wslink=zh:日本三代實錄|chapter=卷第十四}}</ref> The goddess was finally promoted to senior first Rank (正一位) in 1074 ([[:en:Jōhō|Jōhō]] 1).<ref name="Owa221">Ōwa (1990). p. 221-223.</ref>

Stories and claims about the goddess are diverse and contradictory. Regarding her parentage for instance, the lore of Kawaai Shrine (川会神社) in [[:en:Kitaazumi District|Kitaazumi District]] identifies Yasakatome as the daughter of [[:en:Watatsumi|Watatsumi]], god of the sea,<ref>{{cite web |title=八坂刀売神（ヤサカトメノカミ |url=http://japangod.dip.jp/%E5%85%AB%E5%9D%82%E5%88%80%E5%A3%B2%E7%A5%9E.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170625160608/http://japangod.dip.jp/%E5%85%AB%E5%9D%82%E5%88%80%E5%A3%B2%E7%A5%9E.html |archive-date=June 25, 2017 |access-date=May 27, 2017 |website=日本の神様辞典 (Nihon no Kamisama Jiten)}}</ref> which has been seen as hinting to a connection between the goddess and the seafaring [[Azumi people|Azumi clan]] ([[:ja:阿曇氏|安曇氏]]).<ref name="Miyasaka39">Miyasaka (1987). p. 39.</ref> Another claim originating from sources dating from the [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]] is that Yasakatome was the daughter of Ame-no-yasakahiko (天八坂彦命), a god recorded in the ''Kuji Hongi'' as one of the companions of [[List of Japanese deities#Minor kami|Nigihayahi-no-Mikoto]] when the latter came down from heaven.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Ponsonby-Fane |first1=R. A. B. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=UVO4AwAAQBAJ&pg=PT148 |title=Studies in Shinto & Shrines |date=2014 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1136893018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=先代舊事本紀卷第三 天神本紀 |url=https://miko.org/~uraki/kuon/furu/text/sendaikuji/sendaikuji03.htm |website=私本 先代舊事本紀}}</ref><ref name="Miyasaka39" />

Local [[Shinto]] tradition of [[:en:Lake Suwa|Lake Suwa]] holds that the ridges are formed by the gods crossing the lake when traveling between the various buildings of the [[Suwa Grand Shrine]]. Folklore says it is the guardian god of Suwa, [[Takeminakata|Takeminakata-no-kami]], leaving his sanctuary to meet with his wife, the goddess Yasakatome, joining the opposite bank by walking on frozen water.<ref name="Encyclopedia of Shinto - Suwa Shinko2">{{cite web |last1=Takahiro |first1=Nogami |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto - Home : Shrines and Cultic Practices : Suwa Shinkō |url=http://eos.kokugakuin.ac.jp/modules/xwords/entry.php?entryID=1080 |accessdate=25 October 2020 |website=eos.kokugakuin.ac.jp}}</ref> The ice cracks that appear on Lake Suwa during cold winters, the ''omiwatari'' ([[Takeminakata#Omiwatari|see above]]) are reputed in folklore to be caused by Suwa Myōjin's crossing the frozen lake to visit Yasakatome.<ref name="railways1922">{{cite book |author=Japan. Dept. of railways |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=smA0AAAAMAAJ |title=The Hot Springs of Japan (and the Principal Cold Springs) Including Chosen (Korea) Taiwan (Formosa) South Manchuria, Together with Many Tables Giving Classification, Chemical Basis, Curative Values, Radio-activity, Etc. 196 Illustrations, 15 Maps, Specially Drawn, 2 Colored Lithographs |year=1922 |page=194}}</ref>

An alternative explanation for the word ''-tomi'' (as well as the ''-tome'' in 'Yasakatome', the name of this god's consort) is to link it with dialectal words for "snake" (''tomi'', ''tobe'', or ''tōbe''), thereby seeing the name as hinting to the god being a kind of [[Snake worship|serpentine]] water deity (''[[mizuchi]]'').<ref>Tomiku, Takashi (1970). ''Himiko'' (卑弥呼). Gakuseisha. p. 48, cited in Kanai (1982). p. 6.</ref>

